Alright -- don't want to bother you anymore with this. I think you'll post any news concerning FIRMA around here... Good luck!

> In terms of the analysis you are suggesting, the main problem (if I
> understand you correctly) is that you would just look for differences in
> exon expression between the two groups. This will not pick up just
> alternative splicing but also any differential expression at the gene
> level (i.e. common to all exons within the gene).

Yes, the aim of this approach was to just get a picture if there is some alternative expression (maybe due to AS but not necessarily) is going on or not. I thought I could use this to bypass the time until a the publication of the FIRMA paper. And since a limma analysis maybe could be useful to interpret the FIRMA scores (Mark suggested so some time ago) I thought it could be useful to learn something about the limma package anyway. So I'm still hoping for some hints about how to do a limma-test with the plm-estimates (two, unpaired groups. moderatet t-test on the expression estimates on exon or gene level).

> A better route, if you
> want to go this way, is to take the affymetrix approach calculate what
> they call the 'splicing index' -- the difference between the gene and
> exon expression -- and look for group differences there. The SI will not
> be zero centered and also will depend on the gene specific noise, so is
> not itself easy to compare across genes. However, if you are doing a
> t-test this would iron itself out.

